  "diagnosis_text": "The path is h1 \u2192 s4 \u2192 s2 \u2192 s1 \u2192 s3 \u2192 s6 \u2192 h5, with all inter-switch links active. Pings succeed but take 143\u2013344 ms. The five-second TCP test reported no data received, despite 128 KiB reported sent. I\u2019ll check the path\u2019s queues and compare shorter paths to localise the slowdown.**The bottleneck is localised to s2 port 1, transmitting toward s1 port 1**, on the h1 \u2192 h5 path:\n\n`h1 \u2192 s4 \u2192 s2 \u2192 s1 \u2192 s3 \u2192 s6 \u2192 h5`\n\nEvidence:\n- **s2 \u2192 s1 repeatedly transmits at approximately 1 Mbit/s**, with 18\u201320 packets queued and 356\u2013357 queue-overlimit events per two-second sample.\n- Other sampled forward-path ports had **no queue backlog or overlimit events**. No drops or errors were recorded in these samples.\n- Tests avoiding this link were fast: **h1 \u2192 h3 \u224832.4 Gbit/s** and **h7 \u2192 h5 \u224832.6 Gbit/s** in this virtual network. These exercise the source-side and destination-side portions separately.\n- h1 \u2192 h5 pings took **143\u2013344 ms**. The five-second TCP test reported **zero receiver bytes**, although the sender reported 128 KiB; this is not a healthy transfer.\n\n**Likely cause:** an egress traffic-shaping/rate limit around **1 Mbit/s on s2\u2019s port toward s1**, with queueing causing the delay. The measurements strongly support this, but do not expose the configured rate or identify which traffic is filling the queue.\n\n**Next step:** inspect the traffic-control/qdisc and QoS configuration on **`s2-eth1`**, especially its rate, burst and queue limits, and check competing traffic on that egress. I cannot change that configuration with the available tools.",
